@meta
id: 30-04
title: Jump to it, and come back
chapter: 30
type: lesson
skills: ctags, goto-definition
par: 82
minutes: 6
vscode: F12 to go, Alt-Left to come back
vimrc: set hidden
open: src/domain/order/order.provider.ts

@brief
`cancel` writes a `refundReceipt` into the order, but nothing ever sets
`receiptId`, so this file does not compile. The receipt comes back from
the refund call; the refund call currently returns the whole gateway
response.

Two edits, one at each end of the same jump.

  1. At the definition of `issueRefund`, return `response.data.receiptId`
     rather than `response.data`.
  2. Back at the call site, capture it: `const receiptId = await …`.

`Ctrl-]` is `:tag` for the word under the cursor, and `Ctrl-T` is the way
back. They are a stack, not a toggle: jump three definitions deep and
three `Ctrl-T` presses unwind you exactly the way you came.

@keys
| `Ctrl-]` | jump to the definition of the word under the cursor |
| `Ctrl-T` | pop back one level of the tag stack |
| `:tags` | show the stack, deepest last |
| `:pop` | the long form of `Ctrl-T` |

@hints
Search for `issueRefund` to get the cursor on the call, then `Ctrl-]`.
--
At the definition, the line to change is the `return`. `f;` puts you on
the semicolon and `i` types in front of it.
--
`Ctrl-T` brings you back to the exact character you left from, so the
second edit starts with `^` and an insert.

@solution
    /issueRefund<Enter>  Ctrl-]
    /response\.data<Enter>  f; i .receiptId <Esc>
    Ctrl-T  ^ i const receiptId =  <Esc>  :wqa<Enter>

Note what you did not do: you did not know which file `issueRefund` was
in, you did not open a file browser, and you did not lose your place.
That is the whole of F12 and Alt-Left, from a text file that `ctags`
wrote.

`:tags` prints the stack. It is worth looking at once, because it
explains the behaviour that confuses people: `Ctrl-T` is not "the
previous file", it is "one level shallower". If you jump A → B, come
back, then jump A → C, the stack has one entry both times.

`Ctrl-]` needs the cursor on the name, which makes `*`, `f` and `w`
part of the workflow rather than separate from it. Searching for a call
and pressing `Ctrl-]` is two motions and lands you on the definition —
about as fast as the mouse hand moves to the trackpad.

